#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060470} :activity/id #uuid "8089f422-4708-55f1-8234-e77b87f79d18" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048737 :topic/id #uuid "b9ffda55-6967-5939-88c6-796a478b7b95" :topic/math-academy-id 262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060874 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$. Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n> *If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e07c8310-fe8a-5f3e-9ff0-7cd4e93a784c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2383 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f3aaec85-3c9b-585d-9138-8646ce0db776" :step/math-academy-id 10425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059465 :question/id #uuid "b5f19406-37c2-569e-9c9e-b99e00234f91" :question/math-academy-id "e-3084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cc0e4fc-64ca-5c1b-abbe-357e930af7a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060291}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Hypotenuse to the Length Of the Shortest Side"} :step/id #uuid "a6377883-059a-5ebf-97b1-38ef2ad683fd" :step/math-academy-id 10427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060875 :tutorial/content "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x)^{2} &= x^{2} + h^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 4x^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2dbb6c4-dedc-56fa-8e66-87e843a7f7fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2386 :tutorial/title "The Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "860a330d-51b9-5aba-a7ce-25da9f5b8254" :step/math-academy-id 10429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059467 :question/id #uuid "f399fe29-f767-5ea5-bc2b-0d47c0e8fbaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26db86bd-122c-5d1b-9684-9444897d1c44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060293}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Shorter Leg to the Length of the Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "ec4f5b67-5c3e-5ce2-bb3a-7b6b95c2a0c8" :step/math-academy-id 10432 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059640 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059468 :question/id #uuid "40a94955-95e0-5945-9a84-d51b8c691bce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6bd1cf-98e8-5fc8-b605-dd4d24ac8f6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060094}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Hypotenuse to the Length Of the Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "ab1f256a-5f6e-5d12-9b0f-482675761ab1" :step/math-academy-id 10434}] :activity/title "30-60-90 Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059937}]}
